 "drivers.verdict": {
  "computed_at": "2026-09-26T15:32:30+00:00",
  "definition": "Plain-language verdict on what survives from the v1 drivers claims (drought signal, AZ/NV fuel-limited reading, skill number) and what changes under coverage-restriction and forward-in-time testing.",
  "n": null,
  "note": null,
  "source": "analysis.drivers",
  "unit": null,
  "value": "The pooled-OLS skill number replicates almost exactly (17.1% vs v1's 17.1%, OOS R2 0.711 vs 0.711). The drought signal survives: median per-state Spearman rho is -0.45 (negative, PDSI down -> acres up), Colorado stays the strongest single-state signal (rho -0.724), and the model's own z-coefficient on PDSI (-0.1013) is the largest of the three climate terms. The AZ/NV \"fuel-limited\" reading (weak or positive PDSI-acres relationship, drought does not increase burned area because there is little fine fuel to carry fire) holds up under the coverage restriction: AZ rho -0.177 (all years) vs -0.177 (usable years only), NV 0.13 vs 0.13; both stay in the same sign and both stay non-significant (p > 0.05) in both versions. What changes: the model's worst misses are not random, 55% of the top 20 LOYO misses fall in state-years outside the coverage-usable window, against 20% of all rows, so a meaningful share of the model's apparent error is really reporting-coverage noise, not a climate-model failure. The forward-in-time test is the real stress test: an expanding-window fit (train on the past, predict the next year, 2005-2020) gets 14.7% skill and OOS R2 0.690, versus 17.1% and 0.711 for LOYO, a 14% relative drop in skill. Some of the LOYO number comes from interpolating within a year that shares its state-level climatology with 28 other years already in the training set, a form of leakage a genuine forecast would not have; the forward test is the more honest estimate of how well this model would have predicted a new fire season in real time."
 },
